# Scalar SSE Call ABI And Tanh Acceptance

schema_version: 1
directive: `INBOX-20`
target: `x86_64-linux-gnu`
validation_tier: 3
result: pass

The x86_64 SysV planner is the sole call-argument classifier for the new
scalar SSE direct-call path. Half, float, and double arguments use XMM0-XMM7
independently from GPR argument numbering; unsupported stack FP arguments stay
fail-closed. Parallel named FP arguments are staged through the existing frame
and value-location authorities before destination placement. Scalar FP results
bind width-tagged XMM0 locations, and the verifier treats FP calls as defining
XMM0 rather than EAX/RAX. Focused tests cover swapped and multiple FP arguments,
mixed GPR/SSE numbering, a live XMM value across a call, all three result
widths, and call-marker behavior.

`tail` and `notail` are lowered as ordinary calls. `musttail` remains rejected
as `UnsupportedTailCallAbi`; no direct jump or tail-call ABI was added. The
fresh INBOX-19 baseline contains 42 unique tail-diagnostic rows, correcting the
older 44-row estimate. Inspection of the captured IR finds only `tail` markers
in those rows: 42 functions, zero `notail`, and zero `musttail`. Ten rows now
emit after ordinary-call lowering, while 32 expose their actual ABI, type,
frame, or selector frontier. These emissions are not separate semantic claims.
The row-level inventory is
`docs/evidence/std_debug_hello_tail_marker_inventory_inbox20.tsv`.

The real `compiler_rt.tan.tanh` body now emits as an operation- and type-driven
half-to-float conversion, scalar float direct call, and float-to-half
conversion. Its focused object has three PLT32 relocations: `__extendhfsf2`,
the exact dotted symbol `compiler_rt.tan.tanf`, and `__truncsfhf2`. A static
native executable resolves the dotted callee to a controlled local stub and
passes four bit-exact half cases with status 0. No system `tanf` can satisfy the
call, and this does not claim tangent semantics. The real internal
`compiler_rt.tan.tanf` body remains unimplemented at
`UnsupportedCodegenControlFlow / unsupported_instruction / fpext`.

